 ],
 "literal": "Bees swarming, dense vine-clusters interspersing, / like gold-made, beautifully crafted ornaments worn by women / appearing in their tresses, the fresh flowers of the Konrai tree, / even if the forest declares, \"It is the rainy season!\", / as for me, I do not believe it; he is not one to utter lies.",
 "translation": "Though the forest declares, 'The rainy season has come!' by showing the fresh blossoms of the konrai tree—which appear like gold ornaments beautifully crafted and worn in the dark tresses of women, interspersed among dense vine-clusters where bees swarm—as for me, I will not believe it. For he is not one to speak untruths.",
 "modern": "வண்டுகள் மொய்க்குமாறு நெருங்கிய கொடிகளிலுள்ள பூங்கொத்துகளுக்கு இடையே, பெண்கள் அணிந்துள்ள பொன்னால் செய்யப்பட்ட அழகிய அணிகலன்களைப் போலக் கூந்தலில் தோன்றும் புதிய கொன்றை மலர்களைக் கொண்டு, 'கார்காலம் வந்துவிட்டது' என்று இந்தக் காடு கூறினாலும், நான் அதனை நம்பமாட்டேன்; ஏனெனில், அவர் பொய் சொல்பவர் அல்லர்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her companion during the onset of the rainy season. The hero had promised to return from his journey before the rains, but he has not yet arrived. Looking at the forest, the heroine sees the golden konrai flowers blooming amidst the green foliage, resembling golden ornaments in a woman's dark hair. Although the forest's blossoms proclaim that the rainy season has begun, the heroine refuses to believe it. She reasons that her lover is a man of absolute truth, so if he has not returned, it cannot truly be the rainy season yet. This expression of deep trust amidst anxiety is a classic theme of patient waiting (iruttal) in the Mullai (pastoral) landscape.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
